{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":1,"delta":"{\"path\": \"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md\", \"content\": \"# Event projector incident\\n\\nStatus: resolved\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n- A single temporary persistence rejection permanently poisoned the projector:\\n  the shared promise chain stayed rejected, so every subsequent `project()` call\\n  for every trade rejected forever.\\n- State was committed to memory *before* persistence, so a rejected write left\\n  a phantom committed state that was never stored.\\n- One global promise chain serialized all trades: a slow store write for one\\n  trade blocked unrelated trades.\\n- No version or event validation: version gaps, stale events, events for\\n  cancelled trades, and malformed payloads were silently accepted.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\n`project()` chained every event onto one module-level `pending` promise and did\\n`states.set(...)` before `await applyToStore(...)`. A rejection therefore (a)\\nleft the shared chain rejected with no recovery branch, and (b) left uncommitted\\nin-memory state behind. There was also no `reduceEvent` guard for version\\nsequence, terminal status, or payload validity.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n- Per-trade queues (`tails` map keyed by `tradeId`), each recovering from a\\n  prior rejection via `.catch(() => {})` before chaining, so one trade's\\n  failure never blocks or poisons other trades — or its own later events.\\n- In-memory state now commits only after `applyToStore` succeeds; a rejection\\n  leaves the previous state untouched.\\n- `reduceEvent` now enforces: first event must be `CREATED` at version 1;\\n  accepted versions are exactly `current + 1`; duplicate/stale versions return\\n  the current snapshot without persisting; gaps and invalid first events\\n  reject; `CANCELLED` is terminal; `CREATED` requires positive finite\\n  `notional` and non-empty `owner`; `AMENDED` must leave a valid complete\\n  state; unknown/malformed events throw `TypeError`.\\n- Events are deep-cloned on intake and all returned states/snapshots are\\n  `structuredClone` copies, so no mutable internal state escapes.\\n\\n## Regression coverage\\n\\n`student-tests.mjs` adds five tests:\\n\\n1. Different trades progress concurrently while one trade's store write is blocked.\\n2. Duplicate/stale versions return the current snapshot without extra persistence calls.\\n3. Version gaps and invalid first events reject without changing state.\\n4. A `CANCELLED` trade rejects later new versions (terminal).\\n5. Malformed events and invalid field values reject with `TypeError`.\\n6. Returned states, snapshots, and input events do not expose mutable internal state.\\n\\n(The committed `event-projector.test.mjs` covers per-trade serialization and\\nthe rejection-not-poisoning path.)\\n\\n## Remaining risk\\n\\n- The per-trade `tails` map retains an entry per tradeId forever; a projector\\n  handling unbounded distinct trade IDs would grow memory. Entries could be\\n  pruned once a tail settles, at the cost of extra bookkeeping.\\n- `applyToStore` is invoked serially per trade but the store itself is\\n  responsible for its own atomicity across trades; the projector cannot detect\\n  a store that partially applies a write before rejecting.\\n\"}"}}
